{
  "dom_id": "step-q326383",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{7}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $52$ |  |",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16}-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{4})-(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}+\\frac{12\\sqrt{7}}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12\\sqrt{7}}{52}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
